WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.393
You record from two cells.

00:00:01.917 --> 00:00:03.682
Both cells respond to acetylcholine.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.235
- Cell 1 is a frog muscle fibre.

00:00:06.753 --> 00:00:10.004
You record at the end-plate, where the nerve
contacts the muscle.

00:00:10.501 --> 00:00:15.288
- Cell 2 is an atrial myocyte, a muscle cell
from an upper chamber of the heart.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:17.930
You apply acetylcholine to each cell.

00:00:18.446 --> 00:00:20.894
Both cells respond with a current across the
membrane.

00:00:21.556 --> 00:00:23.882
The muscle fibre responds almost at once.

00:00:24.435 --> 00:00:26.503
The heart cell responds much more slowly.

00:00:27.393 --> 00:00:32.715
In both cells, the first event is the same:
acetylcholine binds to a binding site.

00:00:33.289 --> 00:00:34.358
Answer two questions.

00:00:35.135 --> 00:00:39.910
1. What could be different after binding, to
make one response so much slower?

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:45.690
2. Does the delay cost the cell something, give
the cell an advantage, or both?

00:00:46.033 --> 00:00:48.774
Acetylcholine is the same molecule in both
cells.

00:00:49.261 --> 00:00:53.781
So the difference must be in the receptor it
binds, and in the steps after binding.

00:00:54.641 --> 00:00:59.755
At the muscle end-plate, acetylcholine binds a
receptor that is itself an ion channel.

00:01:00.625 --> 00:01:04.851
Colquhoun and Sakmann recorded the current
through single receptors at the frog end-plate.

00:01:05.497 --> 00:01:08.210
Each receptor opened in a burst, with brief
closures.

00:01:08.829 --> 00:01:10.851
Here, binding and response are one event.

00:01:11.431 --> 00:01:12.741
Nothing has to be made first.

00:01:13.258 --> 00:01:18.494
In a heart atrial cell, acetylcholine binds a
different receptor, the muscarinic receptor.

00:01:19.013 --> 00:01:21.178
This is a G-protein-coupled receptor.

00:01:21.662 --> 00:01:26.407
The receptor switches on a G protein, and the G
protein opens a potassium channel.

00:01:27.172 --> 00:01:29.688
Breitwieser and Szabo measured the cost of this
route.

00:01:30.288 --> 00:01:35.215
Before the G protein can bind GTP, it must
release the GDP that it holds.

00:01:35.885 --> 00:01:40.759
On its own, the G protein releases GDP only
about 0.44 times per minute.

00:01:41.376 --> 00:01:44.795
A receptor with acetylcholine bound makes this
release much faster.

00:01:45.459 --> 00:01:49.986
Half of the maximum current is reached at about
160 nM acetylcholine.

00:01:50.401 --> 00:01:52.278
So the receptor route sets the speed.

00:01:52.674 --> 00:01:55.342
The channel responds while acetylcholine is
bound to it.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:02.566
The other route adds a second protein and a
nucleotide exchange between binding and
response, and each step takes time.

00:02:03.196 --> 00:02:05.018
The time gives the cell two things.

00:02:05.518 --> 00:02:06.952
The first is amplification.

00:02:07.539 --> 00:02:12.649
With a protein in the middle, one occupied
receptor can switch on many molecules of the
next protein.

00:02:13.436 --> 00:02:19.549
This is how a short contact outside the cell
becomes a large signal inside, as in the
Cleveland tubes.

00:02:20.078 --> 00:02:23.535
The second is control, because the cell can
regulate the middle step.

00:02:24.187 --> 00:02:26.724
A channel that opens when a ligand binds can do
neither.
